## 2.  Task Model – Tables

| **Name** | **Text** | **Relation** | **Location** | **Number of Robots** | **Justification** |
|----------|----------|--------------|--------------|----------------------|-------------------|
| **AT1: Manipulate Food onto Table** | Robot places the meal onto the inpatient’s table using its manipulation skill. | AND (with G3.1) | Inpatient room (table) | 1 | The robot must be in the room to place the food. |
| **AT2: Instruct Fetcher to Retrieve Meal** | Robot communicates with the chosen fetcher (patient, companion, nurse, or robot) to take the meal from the tray. | AND (with G3.2.2) | Inpatient room (tray) | 1 | Only one robot is needed to give the instruction. |
| **AT3: Indicate Meal to Retrieve** | Robot informs the patient or helper which meal they should pick up. | AND (with G3.3) | Inpatient room | 1 | Direct communication is required before a human can act. |
| **AT4: Log Meal Retrieval** | Robot records the time, meal identity, and location of the retrieval. | AND (with G3.3) | Robot internal system | 1 | Logging is an internal bookkeeping activity. |
| **AT5: Alert Wrong Meal** | Robot notifies the patient or staff if the retrieved meal does not match the order. | AND (with G3.3) | Robot internal system | 1 | Immediate alert is needed to correct the mistake. |
| **AT6: Open Room Door** | Robot opens the inpatient room door (may involve a human or another robot). | AND (with G4) | Room door | 1 | The robot must physically open the door to enter. |
| **AT7: Retrieve Dirty Dishes** | Robot picks up and removes dirty dishes from the patient’s table. | AND (with G5) | Inpatient room | 1 | The robot must be present to collect the dishes. |

